+* `[CTR-8]` `--contracts=off|debug|all` selects where **runtime contract checks**
+  are emitted. The default is `debug` for debug builds, `debug` for release builds,
+  and `off` for shipping builds. This flag is an **independent instrumentation policy**,
+  not a profile and not part of `[PRF-1]`'s semantic exceptions. A contract remains
+  part of the program's declared interface and is still available to static analysis
+  and `[PRV-*]` verification even when its runtime check is omitted. `--contracts=off`
+  therefore means "do not dynamically instrument the contract", never "the contract
+  does not exist". A contract failure is a bug in every setting. A contract discharged
+  by `[PRV-1]` MAY omit its runtime check in every setting. `--contracts=debug` emits
+  checks only for debug-oriented execution; `--contracts=all` emits checks in all build
+  profiles. Runtime contract instrumentation MUST NOT alter accepted source, the
+  meaning of non-failing execution, or the result of ordinary expressions.
+
+* `[CTR-9]` **Dynamic contract dispatch.** For an interface, virtual, `dyn`, or
+  function-value call, the caller checks the contract attached to the statically
+  visible callable type. An overriding implementation MUST accept every state
+  accepted by the visible precondition and MUST satisfy the visible postcondition;
+  it MUST NOT strengthen `@requires` or weaken `@ensures`. An implementation MAY
+  establish stronger postconditions internally, but those are not additional
+  obligations on callers. This rule applies uniformly to direct interface dispatch,
+  virtual dispatch, `dyn` calls and function values. The selected implementation is
+  resolved before its body is analyzed, while the caller's safety obligation comes
+  from the visible callable contract.
 
